## Core Concepts

### 1. Dependency Injection (DI)

**Theory:**
Dependency Injection is a core design pattern that follows the Inversion of Control principle. Instead of a class creating its own dependencies, the dependencies are injected from outside. This inversion allows for flexible, testable, and maintainable code.

**Fundamental Concept:**
The problem DI solves: When class A needs class B, traditionally A creates B internally. This creates tight coupling - A can't work without the exact B implementation. If you want to use B2 instead, you must change A's code. With DI, the container provides B to A. A works with any implementation of B.

**Design Pattern Foundation:**
DI is based on the Dependency Inversion Principle (from SOLID principles):
- High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules
- Both should depend on abstractions
- Abstractions should not depend on details
- Details should depend on abstractions

**Key Points:**
- Objects receive dependencies externally, not creating them
- Enables loose coupling and high testability
- Spring container manages dependency injection
- Constructor injection is the recommended approach
- Supports immutable and final dependencies
- Improves code reusability across projects
- Enables polymorphism and strategy pattern usage
- Separates object creation from business logic
- Facilitates parallel development (mock objects)

**Why Constructor Injection is Best:**
1. Makes dependencies explicit - visible in constructor
2. Ensures immutability - dependencies cannot be null or changed
3. Enables easy testing - pass mocks to constructor
4. Supports required vs optional dependencies clearly
5. Works well with circular dependency detection

**Pattern Explanation:**
```java
// WITHOUT DI - TIGHT COUPLING (Anti-pattern)
public class UserService {
    // Service creates its own dependency - Hard to test
    private UserRepository repository = new UserRepositoryImpl();
    
    public void saveUser(User user) {
        repository.save(user);
    }
    // Problems: Tightly coupled, difficult to test, inflexible
    // Cannot use UserRepositoryMock for testing
    // Cannot use UserRepositoryProductionImpl without code change
}

// WITH DI - LOOSE COUPLING (Best Practice)
@Service
public class UserService {
    private final UserRepository repository; // Immutable dependency
    
    // Constructor Injection (Preferred)
    public UserService(UserRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository; // Dependency provided externally
    }
    
    public void saveUser(User user) {
        repository.save(user);
    }
    // Benefits: Can inject any implementation, easy to test, flexible
}

// Testing with DI
@Test
public void testUserService() {
    // Create mock
    UserRepository mockRepo = mock(UserRepository.class);
    when(mockRepo.save(any())).thenReturn(new User());
    
    // Inject mock
    UserService service = new UserService(mockRepo);
    
    // Test business logic in isolation
    service.saveUser(new User());
}
```

**Key Benefits:**
- **Loose Coupling**: Classes depend on abstractions, not concrete implementations
- **Testability**: Easy to create mock objects for testing
- **Flexibility**: Swap implementations easily (prod vs test, MySQL vs PostgreSQL)
- **Reusability**: Components can be reused with different dependencies
- **Maintainability**: Changes don't ripple through codebase
- **Scalability**: Easy to add new implementations without modifying existing code
- **Dependency Tracking**: Container knows all dependencies automatically

**Three Types of Dependency Injection:**

```java
// 1. Constructor Injection (BEST - Immutable, explicit, testable)
@Service
public class UserService {
    private final UserRepository repository;
    private final EmailService emailService;
    
    public UserService(UserRepository repository, EmailService emailService) {
        this.repository = repository;
        this.emailService = emailService;
    }
    // Immutable - dependencies set once and never change
    // Explicit - constructor shows all dependencies
    // Testable - pass mocks easily
    // Type-safe - compile-time checking
}

// 2. Setter Injection (LESS PREFERRED - mutable, optional)
@Service
public class UserService {
    private UserRepository repository;
    private EmailService emailService;
    
    @Autowired
    public void setRepository(UserRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }
    
    @Autowired
    public void setEmailService(EmailService emailService) {
        this.emailService = emailService;
    }
    // Mutable - dependencies can change after construction (risky)
    // Less obvious - requires reading code to find dependencies
    // Harder to test - must call setters before testing
    // Risk of null dependencies
}

// 3. Field Injection (NOT RECOMMENDED - hidden, hard to test)
@Service
public class UserService {
    @Autowired
    private UserRepository repository;
    
    @Autowired
    private EmailService emailService;
    // Problems: Hard to test without reflection, can be null, not immutable
    // Hidden dependencies - not obvious from constructor
    // Cannot use in immutable objects
    // Fails silently if Spring cannot inject
    // Tight coupling to Spring framework
}
```

### 2. Inversion of Control (IoC) Container

**Theory:**
The IoC Container is the heart of Spring Framework. Instead of your application code creating objects and managing lifecycle, the container takes control. This inversion means the container decides when to create objects, what dependencies to inject, and when to destroy them.

**Fundamental Concept:**
Traditional programming (without IoC): Your code controls the flow and creates objects when needed.
With IoC: Framework controls the flow. You just define configurations, framework creates and manages objects.

This is like the difference between:
- Traditional: You call a library function (you control flow)
- IoC/Framework: Framework calls your code (framework controls flow)

**Container Responsibilities:**
1. Bean Discovery - Scan classpath for components
2. Bean Instantiation - Create instances when needed
3. Dependency Resolution - Inject required dependencies
4. Lifecycle Management - Initialize and destroy beans
5. Configuration - Read external configuration
6. Exception Translation - Convert low-level exceptions

**Key Points:**
- Container controls object lifecycle, not application code
- ApplicationContext is the main container managing beans
- Configuration can be Java-based, XML-based, or annotation-based
- Beans can be created eagerly (at startup) or lazily (on demand)
- Container resolves dependencies automatically
- Container caches beans (singleton by default)
- Support for prototype, singleton, and custom scopes
- Post-processors can intercept bean creation
- Listener pattern for application events
- Integration with AspectJ for AOP

**Container Flow:**
```
1. Container starts (ApplicationContext created)
2. Reads configuration (annotations, XML, properties)
3. Bean Discovery - Finds all @Component, @Service, @Repository, @Bean
4. Bean Registration - Registers bean metadata in registry
5. Dependency Analysis - Identifies all dependencies
6. Bean Instantiation - Creates instances in dependency order
7. Dependency Injection - Injects dependencies into beans
8. Post-Processors - Apply any @PostConstruct or processors
9. Bean Caching - Stores in singleton cache
10. Application Ready - Beans available for use
```

**How It Works:**
```java
@Configuration
public class AppConfig {
    @Bean
    public UserRepository userRepository() {
        // Spring calls this method and registers return value as bean
        return new UserRepositoryImpl();
    }
    
    @Bean
    public UserService userService(UserRepository repository) {
        // Spring automatically injects UserRepository bean
        // Container resolves dependency automatically
        return new UserService(repository);
    }
    
    // Can also use constructor injection
    @Bean
    public EmailService emailService() {
        return new EmailService();
    }
}

// Usage - Container creates and manages beans
ApplicationContext context = new AnnotationConfigApplicationContext(AppConfig.class);

// Get bean from container - Singleton by default
UserService userService1 = context.getBean(UserService.class);
UserService userService2 = context.getBean(UserService.class);
// userService1 == userService2 (same instance)

// Accessing with name
UserRepository repo = context.getBean("userRepository", UserRepository.class);

// Get all beans of type
Map<String, UserRepository> repos = context.getBeansOfType(UserRepository.class);
```

**Bean Lifecycle:**
```
1. Instantiation - Constructor called
2. Properties Set - Dependency injection
3. Aware Callbacks - Spring informs bean about environment
4. Post-Process Before Init - Custom processing before initialization
5. Initialization - @PostConstruct or afterPropertiesSet() called
6. Post-Process After Init - Custom processing after initialization
7. [Bean Ready - Available for use]
8. Pre-Destroy - @PreDestroy or destroy() called on shutdown
9. Bean Destroyed - Removed from container
```

**Key Benefits:**
- Automatic dependency resolution by container (no manual wiring)
- Centralized bean configuration and lifecycle management
- Enables AOP, transaction management, and aspect integration
- Consistent object creation and initialization
- Easy to swap implementations (different beans for different profiles)
- Supports multiple bean instances with different scopes
- Circular dependency detection and reporting
- Performance optimization through lazy initialization

### 3. Bean Lifecycle

**Theory:**
Every Spring bean goes through a well-defined lifecycle from creation to destruction. Understanding this is crucial for initializing resources (database connections), managing state, and cleaning up properly when application shuts down.

**Why Lifecycle Matters:**
- Resource Management: Open connections, database pools, threads at startup
- State Initialization: Load configuration, cache warm-up, pre-computation
- Cleanup: Close connections, release locks, flush caches on shutdown
- Ordering: Ensure beans initialize in correct order
- Hooks: Custom logic at specific points in bean life

**Lifecycle Stages Explained:**

Stage 1: **INSTANTIATION** - Container calls constructor using reflection
- Bean instance created in memory
- No dependencies injected yet
- No post-processors run yet
- Exception: BeanCreationException if constructor fails

Stage 2: **POPULATE PROPERTIES** - All dependencies and fields set
- Constructor injection: Dependencies passed to constructor
- Setter injection: Setters called with dependencies
- Field injection: Fields populated via reflection
- Exception: PropertyAccessException if injection fails

Stage 3: **AWARE INTERFACES** - Spring informs bean about environment
- BeanNameAware.setBeanName() - Bean gets its name
- ApplicationContextAware.setApplicationContext() - Bean gets container reference
- BeanFactoryAware.setBeanFactory() - Bean gets factory reference
- Used to access other beans or container at runtime

Stage 4: **INITIALIZATION** - Final setup before bean is ready
- Post-Process Before Init - Custom processors run (AOP proxies created here)
- @PostConstruct method called - Custom initialization logic
- InitializingBean.afterPropertiesSet() - Alternative initialization
- Custom init-method attribute - Another way to specify init logic

Stage 5: **READY** - Bean is fully initialized and available
- Bean is now in singleton cache
- Can be injected into other beans
- Can be retrieved from context
- Completely operational

Stage 6: **[APPLICATION RUNNING]** - Bean used by application
- Normal operation period
- Can be accessed thousands of times
- Stateless beans returned from cache

Stage 7: **DESTRUCTION** - Bean lifecycle ends on shutdown
- On application shutdown signal received
- Pre-Destroy - @PreDestroy method called
- DisposableBean.destroy() - Custom cleanup logic
- Custom destroy-method attribute - Alternative cleanup

Stage 8: **REMOVED** - Bean removed from memory
- Garbage collector cleans up memory
- Connections closed, resources released
- Bean no longer accessible

**Key Points:**
- **Seven Stages**: Instantiation → Properties → Awareness → Initialization → Usage → Destruction → Removal
- **Lifecycle Hooks**: Special methods called at different stages
- **Initialization Phase**: Setup after bean creation and dependency injection
- **Destruction Phase**: Cleanup before bean removal from container
- **Aware Interfaces**: Let beans know about Spring environment
- Multiple ways to hook into lifecycle
- Order of execution is predictable and documented
- Exceptions in lifecycle can prevent bean initialization
- Lazy-initialized beans have delayed lifecycle

**Complete Lifecycle Flow:**
```
1. INSTANTIATION - Constructor called, bean instance created
2. POPULATE PROPERTIES - Dependencies and fields set
3. AWARE INTERFACES - BeanNameAware, ApplicationContextAware called
4. INITIALIZATION - @PostConstruct or afterPropertiesSet() called
5. READY - Bean available for use in application
6. [Application running...]
7. DESTRUCTION - @PreDestroy or destroy() called on shutdown
8. REMOVED - Bean garbage collected
```

**Practical Example:**
```java
@Component
public class DatabasePool implements InitializingBean, DisposableBean {
    private Connection connection;
    
    // Step 1: Constructor (Instantiation)
    public DatabasePool() {
        System.out.println("1. Constructor - Bean instantiated");
    }
    
    // Step 4a: InitializingBean interface
    @Override
    public void afterPropertiesSet() throws Exception {
        System.out.println("4a. afterPropertiesSet - Initializing resources");
        initializePool();
    }
    
    // Step 4b: @PostConstruct annotation
    @PostConstruct
    public void init() {
        System.out.println("4b. @PostConstruct - Custom initialization");
    }
    
    private void initializePool() {
        System.out.println("Pool initialized with 10 connections");
    }
    
    // Step 6a: DisposableBean interface
    @Override
    public void destroy() throws Exception {
        System.out.println("6a. destroy - Cleaning up resources");
        closePool();
    }
    
    // Step 6b: @PreDestroy annotation
    @PreDestroy
    public void cleanup() {
        System.out.println("6b. @PreDestroy - Final cleanup");
    }
    
    private void closePool() {
        System.out.println("Pool closed, connections released");
    }
}
```

**Output on startup:**
```
1. Constructor - Bean instantiated
4a. afterPropertiesSet - Initializing resources
Pool initialized with 10 connections
4b. @PostConstruct - Custom initialization
[Bean ready to use]
...
6a. destroy - Cleaning up resources
Pool closed, connections released
6b. @PreDestroy - Final cleanup
```

**Multiple Initialization Methods:**
```java
// Method 1: @PostConstruct (Recommended)
@PostConstruct
public void initialize() { }

// Method 2: InitializingBean interface
public void afterPropertiesSet() throws Exception { }

// Method 3: init-method attribute
@Bean(initMethod = "init")
public MyBean myBean() { return new MyBean(); }
```

### 4. Component Scanning

**Theory:**
Component scanning is Spring's automatic bean discovery mechanism. Instead of manually declaring every bean, Spring scans the classpath for classes marked with stereotypes (@Component, @Service, @Repository) and automatically registers them as beans. This reduces boilerplate configuration significantly.

**Fundamental Concept:**
Without component scanning: You must manually declare every bean in XML or @Bean methods.
With component scanning: Annotate classes with @Component, Spring finds and registers automatically.

**How It Works:**
```
1. @SpringBootApplication or @ComponentScan annotation triggers scanning
2. Spring scans base package and all subpackages
3. Looks for classes with stereotype annotations
4. Creates BeanDefinition for each found class
5. Registers in ApplicationContext
6. Instantiates and injects dependencies
```

**Stereotype Annotations - The Core:**
```
@Component      - Generic, base stereotype for any Spring-managed component
@Service        - Indicates service layer (business logic, no special features)
@Repository     - Data access layer (exception translation to DataAccessException)
@Controller     - MVC controller that handles HTTP requests
@RestController - REST endpoint controller (returns data, not views)
@Configuration  - Class defines bean definitions
```

**Key Points:**
- Automatically discovers and registers beans marked with stereotypes
- Scans specified base packages and subpackages recursively
- Reduces need for explicit @Bean declarations
- Can include/exclude classes with filters
- Stereotypes: @Component, @Service, @Repository, @Controller
- Default behavior: Scan same package and subpackages as entry point
- Performance impact: Faster for small applications, negligible for most
- Can be customized with include/exclude filters
- Can specify multiple base packages
- Lazy initialization supported
- Can exclude specific components

**Scanning Scenarios:**

```java
// Scenario 1: Default scanning from Application.java location
@SpringBootApplication  // Application is in com.example package
public class Application {
    // Scans: com.example, com.example.controller, com.example.service, etc.
    // Does NOT scan: com.external (sibling package)
}

// Project structure gets scanned:
// com.example
//   ├── controller/UserController.class      @RestController
//   ├── service/UserService.class            @Service
//   ├── service/EmailService.class           @Service
//   └── repository/UserRepository.class      @Repository

// Scenario 2: Custom package specification
@SpringBootApplication(scanBasePackages = {
    "com.example.service",
    "com.example.repository"
})
public class Application {
    // Only scans: service and repository packages
    // Does NOT scan: controller package
}

// Scenario 3: Explicit @ComponentScan with filters
@Configuration
@ComponentScan(
    basePackages = {"com.example.service", "com.example.repository"},
    includeFilters = @ComponentScan.Filter(
        type = FilterType.ANNOTATION,
        classes = Service.class
    ),
    excludeFilters = @ComponentScan.Filter(
        type = FilterType.REGEX,
        pattern = ".*Test.*"
    )
)
public class AppConfig {
    // Include: Only @Service classes
    // Exclude: Classes with "Test" in name
}
```

**Default Behavior:**
```java
// @SpringBootApplication enables component scanning by default
@SpringBootApplication
public class Application {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(Application.class, args);
    }
    // Automatically scans from com.example and all subpackages
    // where Application.class is located
}

// Project structure gets scanned automatically:
// com.example
//   ├── controller/UserController.class (Found via @RestController)
//   ├── service/UserService.class (Found via @Service)
//   └── repository/UserRepository.class (Found via @Repository)
```

**Controlled Scanning:**
```java
@Configuration
@ComponentScan(
    basePackages = {"com.example.service", "com.example.repository"},
    excludeFilters = @ComponentScan.Filter(
        type = FilterType.REGEX,
        pattern = ".*Test.*"
    )
)
public class AppConfig {
    // Only scans specified packages
    // Excludes test classes
    // More efficient for large projects
}

// Different filter types available:
// FilterType.ANNOTATION - Filter by annotation class
// FilterType.ASSIGNABLE_TYPE - Filter by assignable type
// FilterType.ASPECTJ - Filter by AspectJ expression
// FilterType.REGEX - Filter by class name regex
// FilterType.CUSTOM - Custom filter implementation
```

**Key Benefits:**
- Reduces XML/Java configuration significantly
- Automatic dependency discovery and injection
- Clear intent with stereotype annotations
- Easier maintenance and refactoring
- Less error-prone than manual bean declaration
- Enables automatic wiring through DI
- Supports lazy initialization
- Customizable for complex scenarios

