## Spring Boot Architecture

**Theory:**
Spring Boot follows a layered architecture pattern, one of the most popular patterns in enterprise application development. Each layer has specific responsibilities and communicates through defined interfaces. This separation of concerns makes the application maintainable, testable, and scalable.

**Why Layered Architecture:**
- Separation of concerns: Each layer handles one responsibility
- Testability: Easy to test each layer independently
- Maintainability: Changes in one layer don't affect others
- Reusability: Business logic can be reused (REST, GraphQL, CLI)
- Scalability: Can scale different layers independently
- Team organization: Teams can work on different layers
- Performance: Can cache at each layer boundary

**Layer Responsibilities:**

**Presentation Layer (Controller):**
- HTTP request handling and parsing
- Request validation and conversion
- Response formatting (JSON/XML)
- HTTP status codes and redirect handling
- Authentication/authorization checks
- Input parameter extraction
- Does NOT contain business logic
- Does NOT access database directly

**Service Layer (Business Logic):**
- Core business logic implementation
- Transaction boundaries and management
- Authorization and security checks
- Orchestration between multiple repositories
- Exception handling and transformation
- Validation and data enrichment
- Can be reused across multiple controllers/interfaces
- Does NOT handle HTTP details
- Does NOT access presentation layer

**Repository Layer (Data Access):**
- Database CRUD operations (Create, Read, Update, Delete)
- Query execution and optimization
- ORM mapping (Entity to Database table)
- Transaction coordination
- Data validation at database level
- Does NOT contain business logic
- Does NOT handle HTTP requests
- Abstracts database implementation

**Database Layer:**
- Persistent data storage
- ACID properties enforcement (Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability)
- Indexing and query optimization
- Backup and recovery mechanisms
- Constraint enforcement
- Transaction isolation

**Key Points:**
- Each layer is independent and can be tested separately
- Dependencies flow downward (Controller → Service → Repository)
- Never bypass layers (don't access DB from Controller)
- Each layer has single responsibility (SOLID principle)
- Layers communicate through interfaces/abstraction
- Request flows through all layers downward
- Response flows back through all layers upward

### Layered Architecture

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────┐
│  Presentation Layer (Controllers)   │ ← HTTP Requests Come Here
│  (REST Endpoints, Request/Response)│
├─────────────────────────────────────┤
│  Service Layer (Business Logic)     │ ← Business Rules & Transactions
│  (Validation, Authorization, Flow)  │
├─────────────────────────────────────┤
│  Repository Layer (Data Access)     │ ← Database Operations
│  (JPA, Queries, ORM Mapping)        │
├─────────────────────────────────────┤
│  Database Layer                     │ ← Persistent Storage
│  (MySQL, PostgreSQL, etc.)          │
└─────────────────────────────────────┘
```

### Request Flow in Spring Boot

**Theory:**
Understanding the request flow helps you understand where to put logic and how Spring processes requests. Each step has specific responsibilities and transforms data through the layers.

**Complete Request Lifecycle:**

```
1. HTTP Request arrives at Servlet Container
   ↓ (Browser sends GET /api/users/1)

2. DispatcherServlet receives request
   ↓ (Front Controller pattern - entry point for all requests)

3. HandlerMapping looks up which controller method handles request
   ↓ (Routes based on @GetMapping, @PostMapping, etc.)

4. HandlerAdapter prepares controller method
   ↓ (Resolves parameters, validates, converts types)

5. Interceptors pre-process request (optional)
   ↓ (Authentication, logging, timing)

6. Controller method executes
   ↓ (Processes HTTP request, calls service)

7. Service executes business logic
   ↓ (Validation, authorization, calculations)

8. Repository accesses database
   ↓ (Query execution, ORM mapping)

9. Database executes query and returns data
   ↓ (Raw result set from database)

10. Data flows back through layers
    ↓ (Repository → Service → Controller)

11. Response mapping and serialization
    ↓ (Convert Java objects to JSON/XML)

12. Interceptors post-process response (optional)
    ↓ (Add headers, logging, timing)

13. HTTP Response sent to client
    ↓ (Browser receives JSON response)
```

**Key Points:**
- Request enters through DispatcherServlet (Front Controller)
- Routing is automatic based on annotations
- Each layer adds value and transforms data
- Responses flow back through same layers
- Exceptions can occur at any layer
- Interceptors can intercept before/after each layer
- Data conversion happens at boundaries
- Transactions are managed at service layer
- Performance can be improved at any layer (caching, batching, etc.)

### Key Components:

```java
// 1. Controller Layer
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/users")
public class UserController {
    @PostMapping
    public ResponseEntity<UserDTO> createUser(@RequestBody CreateUserRequest req) {
        // Handle request
        return ResponseEntity.ok(userDTO);
    }
}

// 2. Service Layer
@Service
public class UserService {
    public UserDTO createUser(CreateUserRequest req) {
        // Business logic
        User user = new User(req.getName(), req.getEmail());
        return userRepository.save(user);
    }
}

// 3. Repository Layer
@Repository
public interface UserRepository extends JpaRepository<User, Long> {
    User findByEmail(String email);
}

// 4. Entity/Model Layer
@Entity
@Table(name = "users")
public class User {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;
    
    @Column(unique = true)
    private String email;
    
    private String name;
}
```

