## Auto-Configuration

**Theory:**
Spring Boot auto-configuration automatically configures Spring application based on classpath dependencies. It uses @ConditionalOn* annotations to determine whether to apply configuration. User configuration always takes precedence over auto-configuration, allowing developers to override sensible defaults when needed.

**Fundamental Problem It Solves:**
Without auto-configuration, you'd need to manually declare beans for every dependency:
- DataSource bean for database
- SessionFactory bean for Hibernate
- JdbcTemplate bean for database access
- TransactionManager bean for transactions
- Security filters for Spring Security
- All with correct versions and compatible configurations

Spring Boot: Just add dependency, auto-configuration handles setup.

**Philosophy:**
1. Smart defaults based on conventions
2. User configuration overrides automatically
3. Sensible for 95% of use cases
4. Still customizable when needed

**Key Points:**
- **Smart Defaults**: Sensible configuration for common scenarios
- **Conditional Beans**: Configuration applied only when conditions met
- **User Override**: Custom configuration always takes precedence
- **Explicit Control**: Can exclude auto-configurations when needed
- **META-INF/spring.factories**: Lists auto-configuration classes
- **Conditional Logic**: @ConditionalOn* annotations control application
- **Chain of Configurations**: Multiple auto-configs work together
- **Order Matters**: Some configs depend on others
- **Performance**: Only loads configurations for dependencies present

**How Auto-Configuration Works:**

```
1. Application starts
   ↓ (SpringApplication.run() called)

2. @SpringBootApplication enables auto-configuration
   ↓ (Includes @EnableAutoConfiguration)

3. Spring finds META-INF/spring.factories file
   ↓ (Lists all auto-configuration classes)

4. Lists auto-configuration classes to load
   ↓ (Can be 200+ configurations available)

5. Each auto-config class evaluated with @ConditionalOn*
   ↓ (Check if class on classpath, property exists, etc.)

6. Configuration applied only if conditions met
   ↓ (Skip if not relevant to this application)

7. User config overrides auto-config
   ↓ (Priority: User > Auto)

8. Application ready with all configurations
   ↓ (Beans created, dependencies injected)
```

**Priority Order:**
```
1. User @Configuration classes (Highest)
2. application.properties / application.yml
3. Auto-configuration defaults (Lowest)

User always wins - auto config provides baseline.
```

**Conditional Annotations:**

```java
// Apply only if class exists on classpath
@ConditionalOnClass(JdbcTemplate.class)
// Redis configuration only if spring-data-redis dependency present

// Apply only if class NOT on classpath
@ConditionalOnMissingClass("com.mysql.jdbc.Driver")
// PostgreSQL config only if MySQL not on classpath

// Apply only if specific property exists
