## REST APIs

**Theory:**
REST (Representational State Transfer) APIs are built using HTTP standard methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE) to perform CRUD operations on resources. Spring Boot provides @RestController to easily create REST endpoints that return JSON/XML instead of HTML views. REST emphasizes using HTTP standards correctly rather than creating custom protocols.

**Fundamental Concepts:**

**Resources:**
- Everything is a resource identified by URI
- Resources represent nouns (User, Order, Product)
- Not actions (don't use /getUserById)
- Use hierarchical URIs: /users/1/orders/5

**HTTP Methods (Verbs):**
- GET: Retrieve resource (safe, idempotent) - no side effects
- POST: Create new resource - changes state
- PUT: Replace entire resource - idempotent
- PATCH: Partial update - not idempotent
- DELETE: Remove resource - changes state
- HEAD: Like GET but no body - for headers only
- OPTIONS: Describe communication options - safe

**Idempotency:**
- GET: Idempotent (same result every call)
- POST: Not idempotent (creates new each time)
- PUT: Idempotent (same result, updates same resource)
- DELETE: Idempotent (removing again has same effect)
- PATCH: Not necessarily idempotent

**Key Points:**
- **Resources**: Everything is a resource (User, Order, Product)
- **HTTP Methods**: GET (retrieve), POST (create), PUT (update), DELETE (remove)
- **Status Codes**: 200 (OK), 201 (Created), 204 (No Content), 400 (Bad Request), 404 (Not Found), 500 (Server Error)
- **Request/Response**: JSON body for data exchange
- **Stateless**: Each request contains all needed information
- **HATEOAS**: Can include links to related resources
- **Content Negotiation**: Support multiple formats (JSON, XML)
- **Versioning**: API versions in URL or header
- **Error Handling**: Consistent error response format
- **Documentation**: API contract via Swagger/OpenAPI

**URI Design Best Practices:**
```
✓ /api/v1/users                   - List users
✓ /api/v1/users/{id}              - Get specific user
✓ /api/v1/users/{id}/orders       - User's orders
✓ /api/v1/users/{id}/orders/{oid} - Specific user's order

✗ /api/getUser                     - Using action instead of method
✗ /api/users/get                   - Redundant
✗ /api/user_list                   - Inconsistent naming
```

**HTTP Methods Mapping:**

```java
// GET - Retrieve resource(s)
@GetMapping("/api/users")                // Get all users
@GetMapping("/api/users/{id}")           // Get single user

// POST - Create new resource
@PostMapping("/api/users")                // Create new user

// PUT - Update entire resource
@PutMapping("/api/users/{id}")           // Replace entire user

// DELETE - Remove resource
@DeleteMapping("/api/users/{id}")        // Delete user

// PATCH - Partial update
@PatchMapping("/api/users/{id}")         // Partial update of user
```

**Complete REST Controller Example:**

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/v1/users")
public class UserController {
    
    private final UserService userService;
    
    @Autowired
    public UserController(UserService userService) {
        this.userService = userService;
    }
    
    // GET all users with pagination
    @GetMapping
    public ResponseEntity<Page<UserDTO>> getAllUsers(
            @RequestParam(defaultValue = "0") int page,
            @RequestParam(defaultValue = "10") int size) {
        Page<UserDTO> users = userService.getAllUsers(page, size);
        return ResponseEntity.ok(users);
    }
    
    // GET single user - returns 200 if found, 404 if not
    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    public ResponseEntity<UserDTO> getUserById(@PathVariable Long id) {
        UserDTO user = userService.getUserById(id);
        return ResponseEntity.ok(user);
    }
    
    // POST create new user - returns 201 Created
    @PostMapping
    public ResponseEntity<UserDTO> createUser(@Valid @RequestBody CreateUserRequest req) {
        UserDTO user = userService.createUser(req);
        return ResponseEntity
                .status(HttpStatus.CREATED)
                .header("Location", "/api/v1/users/" + user.getId())
                .body(user);
    }
    
    // PUT update user - returns 200 OK
    @PutMapping("/{id}")
    public ResponseEntity<UserDTO> updateUser(
            @PathVariable Long id,
            @Valid @RequestBody UpdateUserRequest req) {
        UserDTO user = userService.updateUser(id, req);
        return ResponseEntity.ok(user);
    }
    
    // DELETE user - returns 204 No Content
    @DeleteMapping("/{id}")
    public ResponseEntity<Void> deleteUser(@PathVariable Long id) {
        userService.deleteUser(id);
        return ResponseEntity.noContent().build();
    }
}
