## Complete Interview Questions & Answers

### Beginner Level (5 Questions)

**1. What is Spring Boot and why use it?**

**Answer:**
Spring Boot is an opinionated framework built on top of Spring that simplifies Spring application development. 

**Key reasons to use it:**
- **Faster development**: Reduces boilerplate code and configuration
- **Embedded servers**: Tomcat, Jetty, or Undertow built-in - no need for external application server
- **Auto-configuration**: Intelligent defaults based on classpath dependencies
- **Production-ready**: Built-in metrics, health checks, and monitoring via Actuator
- **Microservices**: Easy to build containerized, cloud-native applications
- **Standalone applications**: Create JAR files that run independently

**Example:**
```java
@SpringBootApplication
public class Application {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(Application.class, args);
        // That's it! Server starts automatically on port 8080
    }
}
```

---

**2. What is the difference between Spring and Spring Boot?**

**Answer:**

| Feature | Spring | Spring Boot |
|---------|--------|------------|
| **Configuration** | Manual XML or Java config | Auto-configuration with defaults |
| **Server** | Requires external application server | Embedded server (Tomcat, Jetty) |
| **Dependencies** | Manual management | Managed via starters |
| **Setup Time** | Longer setup | Faster setup |
| **Boilerplate** | More boilerplate code | Minimal boilerplate |
| **Monitoring** | Need to add manually | Built-in Actuator |

**Spring (Old way):**
```xml
<!-- lots of XML configuration -->
<bean id="userService" class="com.example.UserService">
    <constructor-arg ref="userRepository"/>
</bean>
```

**Spring Boot (New way):**
```java
@Service
public class UserService {
    public UserService(UserRepository userRepository) {
        // Auto-wired, auto-configured
    }
}
```

---

**3. What is auto-configuration in Spring Boot?**

**Answer:**
Auto-configuration is Spring Boot's mechanism to automatically configure the application based on classpath dependencies. It uses conditional annotations to decide which beans to create.

**How it works:**
1. Spring Boot scans META-INF/spring.factories for auto-configuration classes
2. Each auto-config class has @ConditionalOn* annotations
3. Only configurations meeting conditions are applied
4. User configuration always overrides auto-configuration

**Key annotations:**
- `@ConditionalOnClass`: Apply if class exists on classpath
- `@ConditionalOnMissingBean`: Apply if bean doesn't exist
- `@ConditionalOnProperty`: Apply if property is set

**Example:**
```java
@Configuration
@ConditionalOnClass(RedisTemplate.class)
@ConditionalOnProperty(name = "spring.redis.enabled", havingValue = "true")
public class RedisAutoConfiguration {
    @Bean
    @ConditionalOnMissingBean
    public RedisTemplate<String, Object> redisTemplate() {
        // Only created if Redis is on classpath and enabled
        return new RedisTemplate<>();
    }
}
```

---

**4. How does dependency injection work in Spring Boot?**

**Answer:**
Dependency Injection (DI) means dependencies are provided externally rather than created internally. Spring container manages this.

**How it works:**
1. Mark class with `@Component`, `@Service`, `@Repository`
2. Spring registers bean in container
3. Inject via constructor, setter, or field
4. Container injects when needed

**Three ways to inject:**

```java
// 1. Constructor Injection (BEST - Immutable)
@Service
public class UserService {
    private final UserRepository repository;
    
    public UserService(UserRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }
}

// 2. Setter Injection
@Service
public class UserService {
    private UserRepository repository;
    
    @Autowired
    public void setRepository(UserRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }
}

// 3. Field Injection (NOT RECOMMENDED)
@Service
public class UserService {
    @Autowired
    private UserRepository repository;
}
```

**Benefits:**
- Loose coupling
- Easy to test with mocks
- Flexible - can swap implementations
- Maintains single responsibility

