**16. What is the difference between @Component, @Service, and @Repository?**

**Answer:**

| Annotation | Layer | Purpose | Special Feature |
|-----------|-------|---------|-----------------|
| **@Component** | Generic | Any Spring-managed component | None |
| **@Service** | Service | Business logic | Marks service layer |
| **@Repository** | Repository | Data access | Translates JPA exceptions |

```java
// @Component - Generic stereotype
@Component
public class MyComponent {
    public void doSomething() { }
}

// @Service - Business logic layer
@Service
public class UserService {
    @Autowired
    private UserRepository userRepository;
    
    public UserDTO createUser(CreateUserRequest request) {
        // Business logic here
        User user = new User(request.getName(), request.getEmail());
        return userRepository.save(user);
    }
}

// @Repository - Data access layer (special exception translation)
@Repository
public interface UserRepository extends JpaRepository<User, Long> {
    User findByEmail(String email);
}

// Why @Repository is special:
/*
When repository throws JPA exceptions like:
- EntityNotFoundException
- PersistenceException

@Repository translates them to Spring's:
- EmptyResultDataAccessException
- DataAccessException

This allows service layer to handle Spring exceptions,
not JPA-specific exceptions.
*/
```

**Layered architecture:**
```
┌─────────────────────┐
│   Controller        │ @RestController
├─────────────────────┤
│   Service           │ @Service (Business Logic)
├─────────────────────┤
│   Repository        │ @Repository (Data Access)
├─────────────────────┤
│   Database          │
└─────────────────────┘
```

