**19. What is the difference between EAGER and LAZY loading?**

**Answer:**
Loading strategies for entity relationships:

```
EAGER  - Load immediately with parent
LAZY   - Load only when accessed
```

**Defaults:**
- OneToOne, ManyToOne: EAGER (default)
- OneToMany, ManyToMany: LAZY (default)

**Examples:**
```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "users")
public class User {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue
    private Long id;
    
    private String name;
    
    // EAGER - Loads orders immediately
    @OneToMany(
        mappedBy = "user",
        fetch = FetchType.EAGER,  // Load immediately
        cascade = CascadeType.ALL
    )
    private List<Order> orders = new ArrayList<>();
    
    // LAZY - Loads addresses only when accessed
    @OneToMany(
        mappedBy = "user",
        fetch = FetchType.LAZY,   // Load on demand
        cascade = CascadeType.ALL
    )
    private List<Address> addresses = new ArrayList<>();
}

// Usage showing difference:
@Service
public class UserService {
    
    @Autowired
    private UserRepository userRepository;
    
    public void demonstrateLoading(Long userId) {
        // Query user
        User user = userRepository.findById(userId).orElseThrow();
        
        // EAGER loading - Orders already loaded
        System.out.println(user.getOrders().size()); // No extra query
        
        // LAZY loading - Will execute query when accessed
        System.out.println(user.getAddresses().size()); // Extra query here!
    }
    
    // N+1 Query Problem with LAZY loading
    public void badExample() {
        List<User> users = userRepository.findAll(); // Query 1
        
        for (User user : users) {
            System.out.println(user.getAddresses()); // Query N (one per user!)
        }
        // Total: 1 + N queries = N+1 problem!
    }
    
    // Solution: Use JOIN FETCH
    @Query("SELECT u FROM User u LEFT JOIN FETCH u.addresses WHERE u.id = :id")
    User findUserWithAddresses(@Param("id") Long id);
}
```

**EAGER vs LAZY comparison:**
```
EAGER:
- Pros: No N+1 problem, data immediately available
- Cons: Loads unwanted data, slower initial query

LAZY:
- Pros: Only loads what's needed, faster initial query
- Cons: Risk of N+1 problem, LazyInitializationException outside transaction
```

**Best practices:**
- Use LAZY by default
- Use EAGER only for commonly accessed relationships
- Use JOIN FETCH to avoid N+1 problems

