**20. How do you implement caching in Spring Boot?**

**Answer:**
Caching improves performance by storing frequently accessed data:

```java
// 1. Enable caching
@Configuration
@EnableCaching
public class CacheConfig {
    
    @Bean
    public CacheManager cacheManager() {
        // In-memory caching (development)
        return new ConcurrentMapCacheManager("users", "products");
    }
    
    // Or use Redis for production
    @Bean
    public CacheManager redisCacheManager() {
        return RedisCacheManager.create(redisConnectionFactory());
    }
}

// 2. Use caching annotations
@Service
public class UserService {
    
    @Autowired
    private UserRepository userRepository;
    
    // Cache result by id
    @Cacheable(value = "users", key = "#id")
    public UserDTO getUserById(Long id) {
        System.out.println("Fetching user from DB: " + id);
        return userRepository.findById(id)
                .map(this::convertToDTO)
                .orElseThrow();
    }
    
    // Update and invalidate specific cache entry
    @CacheEvict(value = "users", key = "#id")
    public UserDTO updateUser(Long id, UpdateUserRequest request) {
        User user = userRepository.findById(id).orElseThrow();
        user.setName(request.getName());
        userRepository.save(user);
        return convertToDTO(user);
    }
    
    // Update cache without invalidating
    @CachePut(value = "users", key = "#user.id")
    public UserDTO saveUser(User user) {
        User saved = userRepository.save(user);
        return convertToDTO(saved);
    }
    
    // Clear all cache entries
    @CacheEvict(value = "users", allEntries = true)
    public void refreshAllUsers() {
        System.out.println("Cache cleared");
    }
    
    // Conditional caching
    @Cacheable(
        value = "users",
        key = "#id",
        condition = "#id > 0"  // Cache only if id > 0
    )
    public UserDTO getUser(Long id) {
        return userRepository.findById(id)
                .map(this::convertToDTO)
                .orElseThrow();
    }
    
    // Custom cache key
    @Cacheable(
        value = "users",
        key = "#name.concat('_').concat(#email)"
    )
    public UserDTO getUserByNameEmail(String name, String email) {
        return userRepository.findByNameAndEmail(name, email)
                .map(this::convertToDTO)
                .orElseThrow();
    }
}

// 3. Cache configuration properties
/*
application.yml:
spring:
  cache:
    type: simple          # or redis, caffeine, etc.
    cache-names: users,products
    
    redis:
      time-to-live: 600000  # 10 minutes in milliseconds
    
    caffeine:
      spec: maximumSize=500,expireAfterWrite=10m
*/

// 4. Controller usage
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/users")
public class UserController {
    
    @Autowired
    private UserService userService;
    
    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    public ResponseEntity<UserDTO> getUser(@PathVariable Long id) {
        // First call: Fetches from DB, caches result
        // Second call: Returns from cache (no DB query)
        return ResponseEntity.ok(userService.getUserById(id));
    }
}

// 5. Demonstrating cache behavior
@Component
public class CacheDemonstration {
    
    @Autowired
    private UserService userService;
    
    public void demonstrateCaching() {
        // First call - DB query
        long start = System.currentTimeMillis();
        UserDTO user1 = userService.getUserById(1L);
        long time1 = System.currentTimeMillis() - start;
        System.out.println("First call: " + time1 + "ms (DB query)");
        
        // Second call - Cache hit
        start = System.currentTimeMillis();
        UserDTO user2 = userService.getUserById(1L);
        long time2 = System.currentTimeMillis() - start;
        System.out.println("Second call: " + time2 + "ms (From cache)");
        // time2 will be much faster than time1
    }
}
```

**Caching strategies:**
- `@Cacheable`: Cache result, return from cache if exists
- `@CacheEvict`: Remove from cache
- `@CachePut`: Always execute, update cache
- `@Caching`: Combine multiple cache operations

**Cache backends:**
- Simple (in-memory)
- Redis (distributed)
- Caffeine (high-performance)
- Hazelcast (clustered)

